## What `read_agent` can actually see

Verified. Do not waste steps rediscovering these.

**Works (via `sudo`, no password):**
- `systemctl status <unit>`, `systemctl --failed`, `systemctl list-timers`
- `journalctl --no-pager …`, `journalctl -u <unit> --no-pager …`
- `ss -tlnp`, `lsof -i`
- `cat /var/log/fail2ban.log`, `cat /var/log/unattended-upgrades/*`
- `crontab -l -u <user>`
- On `dockassist`: `docker ps|logs|inspect|stats|system df`
- On `cwwk`: `qm list|status|config`, `pct list|status|config`, `zpool status|list|iostat`, `zfs list|get`, `sensors`, `pvesh get`, `dmesg`

**Monitoring logs and check scripts** (this is usually where the answer to a
"Script Failed" alert is — reach for it early):
- `sudo agent_read list-logs` / `sudo agent_read list-scripts` — list what is
  available on that host.
- `sudo agent_read log <name>` — the actual output of a monitoring check, e.g.
  `sudo agent_read log tado_health_check.log`. This is what a per-service
  wrapper wrote when it failed.
- `sudo agent_read script <name>` — the check script itself, e.g.
  `sudo agent_read script check_audio_output.sh`, so you can see exactly what
  it tests and why it might have failed.
- Names are validated: pass a bare filename, not a path (no `/`, no `..`).

**Home Assistant entity state** (on `dockassist` only):
- `sudo ha_state <entity_id>` — read-only current state of one HA entity, e.g.
  `sudo ha_state climate.master_bedroom` or `sudo ha_state sun.sun`. Use this
  to confirm whether a device a Slack alert named is *actually* offline right
  now. It is GET-only — you cannot change anything. Do **not** curl HA's API
  directly with no token: that returns 401 and HA logs it as a failed login,
  which itself fires an alert.

**Does not work — expect these to fail:**
- **Other files under `/home/choco`** — the home is `0700`. The two read
  helpers above are the *only* window into it; a plain `cat /home/choco/...`
  still fails. So `.tado_tokens`, `secrets.yaml`, `.ssh/`, and anything outside
  `.logs`/`.scripts` remain invisible. That is deliberate.
- `pct exec` on cwwk — not permitted, so you cannot reach inside containers.
- Any write, restart, or config change anywhere. By design.

To check whether a host's own monitoring is still running, read the cron
journal (`sudo journalctl -u cron --no-pager -o short-unix`) and look for
recent `enhanced_monitoring_wrapper` invocations — that tells you it *ran*. To
find out whether it *passed*, read its log with `sudo agent_read log <name>`.

## Monitoring already in place

Do not propose rebuilding any of this.

- Every host runs `enhanced_monitoring_wrapper` from cron, wrapping per-service
  checks. Failures post to `#home-alerts`; heartbeats and recoveries go to
  `#home-logging`.
- Several checks self-heal (restart a unit) before alerting. An alert that
  cleared on its own is expected behavior, not a mystery.
- Backups run to Cloudflare R2, with healthchecks.io freshness pings.
- Your own Tier 1 sweep runs hourly at :{{ agent_sweep_minute }} and is the
  thing that produced the anomaly you are investigating.

